<%#
  What is actually executing this strategy, in the place the user asks the
  question. "Why is nothing happening?" is answered by the terminal's state and
  by nothing else on this page, and a user who has to read an event log to find
  out that their broker password was wrong has been failed by the interface.

  connection: whatever is reporting for this user right now, or nil
  instance: the user's managed terminal, or nil
  credential: their broker details, or nil
  diagnostics: what the terminal last logged about a problem (see
    Mt5::Runtime::Local#diagnostics), shown when it is not connected

  `connection` is checked FIRST and on its own. A terminal the user runs
  themselves with an API token has no managed instance and no stored broker
  credentials, so judging by those alone declared a terminal that was reporting
  every ten seconds to be absent — on the page whose whole job is to answer
  "is anything running this?", while the sidebar two inches away said it was
  connected. Reporting is the evidence. Who started the terminal is not.
%>
<% if connection&.live? %>
  <%= render "components/alert", variant: :success,
             title: "Your terminal is connected",
             body: "#{connection.broker.presence || 'Your terminal'} is reporting " \
                   "#{connection.symbol.presence || 'this symbol'}, last heard from " \
                   "#{time_ago_in_words(connection.last_report_at)} ago. " \
                   "Everything below comes from it." %>
<% elsif instance&.healthy? %>
  <%# Connected and reporting. The figures on this page are real, so nothing
      is claimed here that the status indicator does not already show. %>
<% elsif instance.nil? && connection.present? %>
  <%# A terminal the user runs themselves reported once and then stopped.
      That is a different problem from never having connected, and it has a
      different fix. A managed terminal's own state, below, says more than
      this ever could, so it is only for terminals we do not run. %>
  <%= render "components/alert", variant: :warning,
             title: "Your terminal has stopped reporting",
             body: "The last report was #{time_ago_in_words(connection.last_report_at)} ago. " \
                   "Check that MetaTrader is still open, that Algo Trading is on, and that " \
                   "the Expert Advisor is still attached to the chart." %>
<% elsif instance.nil? && credential.nil? %>
  <% unconnected = capture do %>
    Nothing is executing this strategy, so no positions, prices or profit are shown —
    there are none to show. Add your
    <%= link_to "broker account", edit_broker_credential_path, class: "link" %>
    and the platform will run a terminal for you, or run MetaTrader yourself with an
    <%= link_to "API token", api_tokens_path, class: "link" %>.
  <% end %>
  <%= render "components/alert", variant: :warning,
             title: "No terminal is connected", body: unconnected %>
<% elsif instance.nil? %>
  <%= render "components/alert", variant: :info,
             title: "No terminal yet",
             body: "Your broker details are saved. A terminal is started for you when you start this strategy." %>
<% elsif instance.failed? %>
  <% failed_body = capture do %>
    <p><%= instance.last_error_message.presence || "The terminal failed and has not been able to restart." %></p>
    <% if instance.crash_looping? %>
      <p class="mt-2">
        It has failed <%= instance.consecutive_failures %> times in a row, so automatic restarts are paused.
        Fix anything listed below, then retry.
      </p>
    <% elsif instance.next_restart_at&.future? %>
      <p class="mt-2">The next automatic attempt is in <%= distance_of_time_in_words_to_now(instance.next_restart_at) %>.</p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render "strategies/terminal_diagnostics", instance:, diagnostics: %>
  <% end %>
  <%= render "components/alert", variant: :danger,
             title: instance.crash_looping? ? "Your terminal keeps failing" : "Your terminal stopped with an error",
             body: failed_body %>
<% elsif instance.running? %>
  <%# Marked running, but no heartbeat within the timeout: the terminal has died or hung
      without the platform being told. Its open positions have no one managing them, so
      this is shown as a problem, not as a waiting state. %>
  <% stale_body = capture do %>
    <p>
      Last heard from <%= instance.last_heartbeat_at ? "#{time_ago_in_words(instance.last_heartbeat_at)} ago" : "never" %>.
      Positions it opened are not being managed until it reports again, so check them at your broker.
    </p>
    <%= render "strategies/terminal_diagnostics", instance:, diagnostics: %>
  <% end %>
  <%= render "components/alert", variant: :danger,
             title: "Your terminal has stopped reporting",
             body: stale_body %>
<% elsif instance.connecting? || instance.starting? %>
  <% waiting_body = capture do %>
    <p>
      It is not reporting yet, so no positions, prices or profit are shown.
      A terminal normally takes a minute or two to sign in to a broker.
    </p>
    <%= render "strategies/terminal_diagnostics", instance:, diagnostics: %>
  <% end %>
  <%= render "components/alert", variant: :info,
             title: "Your terminal is #{instance.state}",
             body: waiting_body %>
<% else %>
  <% stopped_body = capture do %>
    <p>It is not reporting, so no positions, prices or profit are shown.</p>
    <%= render "strategies/terminal_diagnostics", instance:, diagnostics: %>
  <% end %>
  <%= render "components/alert", variant: :info,
             title: "Your terminal is #{instance.state}", body: stopped_body %>
<% end %>
